==Introduction==

===Abstract===

This BIP defines Tapscript CHECKSIG Multiplexer (CHECKSIGMUX) with leaf version 0xc2, a dialect of Tapscript that enables scripts to verify signatures from an extensible collection of digital signature schemes by redefining Tapscript's signature verification opcodes (sigops). Public keys are prefixed in scripts with a one-byte scheme flag that identifies the corresponding signature scheme and the verification algorithm to use for signature operations involving that key. Signature verification against a public key prefixed by a reserved flag byte succeeds unconditionally, providing an upgrade path for other digital signature schemes in the future. Requirements are specified for future BIPs defining new candidate schemes. Importantly, Tapscript CHECKSIGMUX also modifies resource limits relative to Tapscript leaf version 0xc0, such as the maximum stack element size and the sigops budget accounting mechanism. A discussion of relevant trade-offs and justifications for these design choices is provided.

==Motivation==

Bitcoin's digital signatures currently rely on the computational hardness of the elliptic curve discrete logarithm problem (ECDLP) in the group of points on the secp256k1 elliptic curve. Threats to the soundness of this assumption would necessitate adding new signature schemes, but today this is a very hard problem.

Bitcoin signature verification is currently bound to digital signature schemes that depend on this problem remaining hard. For example, Taproot ([https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0341.mediawiki BIP341]) key-path spends and script-path spends using Tapscript leaf version 0xc0 rules ([https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0342.mediawiki BIP 342]), verify all signatures using the Schnorr signature verification algorithm ([https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0340.mediawiki BIP 340]). Legacy and SegWit v0 Bitcoin signature verifications are bound to ECDSA on secp256k1. This coupling has meant that introducing new digital signature schemes to Bitcoin has required a combination of new output types, new SegWit versions, and/or ad-hoc opcode redefinition. A more flexible architecture that decouples verification from any single scheme would make it easier to adopt new primitives in the future — whether for post-quantum security, efficiency, or other desirable properties — without proliferating special‑purpose script versions.

Leaf version 0xc2 decouples the operation of signature verification from any particular digital signature scheme. Scripts commit to public keys that declare their scheme via a flag byte. The script interpreter uses this flag byte to determine the correct signature verification algorithm to apply to a given signature. The digital signature scheme for each flag byte is defined in a separate BIP, allowing the signature ecosystem to evolve without requiring changes to script system semantics.

With CHECKSIGMUX a user may commit an output to multiple keys, each potentially using a different signature scheme, inside the leaves of a script tree. At spend-time the user enjoys their choice of signing scheme. In this way users can design more expressive spending access structures as new signature schemes with different properties are deployed.

==Specification==

===Overview===

This BIP defines a new Tapscript leaf version 0xc2, in which all sigops -- signature verification opcodes like <code>OP_CHECKSIG</code>, <code>OP_CHECKSIGADD</code>, and <code>OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY</code> -- are redefined to support multiple digital signature schemes in the same script and/or script tree. The exact signature verification algorithm to perform is determined in scripts by the single-byte scheme prefix prepended to the public key. The script interpreter detects this byte during each signature operation and then performs the indicated signature verification algorithm on the given public key and a provided signature.

Script validation under Tapscript leaf version 0xc2 follows the rules for Tapscript leaf version 0xc0 as specified in BIP 342<ref>https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0342.mediawiki</ref>, except for the following modifications:

# '''Applicability.''' These modified script validation rules apply to tapscript leaf version 0xc2 on any SegWit output supporting Tapscript including P2TR. For script validations using these modified rules, replace all references to leaf version 0xc0 in BIP 342 with leaf version 0xc2. If the output type is not P2TR, references to BIP 341 with the corresponding specification BIP for the enclosing output type. The enclosing output type spec must supply the script and initial stack for execution.
# '''Signature opcode semantics.''' The rules in BIP 342's "Rules for signature opcodes" and "Signature validation" sections are replaced in their entirety by the rules in the "Signature Verification / Sigop Modifications" section of this BIP.
# '''Resource limits.''' The maximum stack element size is raised from 520 bytes to 6000 bytes, and the sigops budget accounting mechanism is modified. These changes are specified in the "Resource Limits" section. All other resource limits of BIP 342, such as the 1000 item stack size limit, apply unchanged.

All other rules of BIP 342 — including the decoding of tapscripts, the <code>OP_SUCCESSx</code> opcodes, disabled opcodes, consensus-enforced MINIMALIF, and the script execution rules — apply verbatim.

===Public Key Format===

A well-formed public key in Tapscript leaf version 0xc2 is a non-empty byte string interpreted as:

<pre>
flag || pubkey_data
</pre>

where <code>flag</code> is the first byte and <code>pubkey_data</code> is the remainder (possibly empty).

===Signature Verification / Sigop Modifications===

"Sigops" are the signature verification opcodes used to verify signatures in Bitcoin script. These opcodes, <code>OP_CHECKSIG</code>, <code>OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY</code>, and <code>OP_CHECKSIGADD</code> are redefined in Tapscript leaf version 0xc2 as follows.

In the procedures below, "fail" means script execution MUST fail and terminate immediately, rendering the spend invalid. The term "continue" means the current signature operation is complete, and script execution proceeds with the next opcode.

====OP_CHECKSIG====

# Pop two elements <code>pubkey</code> (top element) and <code>signature</code> (second-to-top element) from the stack.
#* If fewer than two elements are available on the stack, fail.
# If <code>pubkey</code> size is zero, fail.
# If <code>signature</code> is empty, push the empty vector and continue.
# Let <code>flag</code> be the first byte of <code>pubkey</code>, and <code>pubkey_data</code> be the remainder.
# If <code>flag</code> is reserved (i.e. not a defined scheme flag), push <code>0x01</code> and continue.
# Verify <code>signature</code> against <code>pubkey_data</code> and the transaction data using the signature verification algorithm defined for <code>flag</code>.
#* Let <code>ok</code> be the boolean verification result.
#* Let <code>sigops_cost</code> be the sigops cost returned by the verify algorithm.
# Decrement the sigops budget by <code>sigops_cost</code> and fail if this budget falls below zero.
# If not <code>ok</code>, fail.
# Push <code>0x01</code> and continue.

====OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY====

# Execute as <code>OP_CHECKSIG</code> above.
# Pop the <code>result</code> from the top of the stack; if <code>result</code> is false, fail. Otherwise continue.

====OP_CHECKSIGADD====

# Pop three elements <code>pubkey</code>, then <code>n</code>, then <code>signature</code> from the stack.
#* If fewer than three elements are on the stack, the script MUST fail and terminate immediately.
# If <code>pubkey</code> is empty, fail.
# If <code>n</code> is not a valid CScriptNum of at most 4 bytes, fail.
# If <code>signature</code> is empty, push <code>n</code> and continue.
# Let <code>flag</code> be the first byte of <code>pubkey</code>, and <code>pubkey_data</code> be the remainder.
# If <code>flag</code> is reserved (i.e. not a defined scheme flag), push <code>n + 1</code> and continue.
# Verify <code>signature</code> against <code>pubkey_data</code> and the transaction data using the signature verification algorithm defined for <code>flag</code>.
#* Let <code>ok</code> be the boolean verification result.
#* Let <code>sigops_cost</code> be the sigops cost returned by the verify algorithm.
# Decrement the sigops budget by <code>sigops_cost</code> and fail if this budget falls below zero.
# If not <code>ok</code>, fail.
# Push <code>n + 1</code> and continue.

As with Tapscript version 0xc0 the sigops <code>OP_CHECKMULTISIG</code> and <code>OP_CHECKMULTISIGVERIFY</code> are disabled in Tapscript version 0xc2. The disabled opcodes behave in the same way as <code>OP_RETURN</code>: fail and terminate the script immediately when executed. They are ignored when found in an unexecuted branch of the script.

===Threshold and Multi-Signature Scripts===

Threshold (m-of-n) and multi (n-of-n) signatures are realized by combining <code>OP_CHECKSIGADD</code> with existing arithmetic opcodes (e.g. <code>OP_ADD</code>, <code>OP_NUMEQUAL</code>), following the same pattern used in BIP 342.

Since each public key commits to its own scheme flag, a multi-signature script may consist of keys of different schemes verified by different signature verification algorithms. Each signer need not know or care which scheme(s) their co-signers use.

===Defined Scheme Flags===

No signature scheme flags are defined in this BIP. Additional BIPs must define signature schemes for use with CHECKSIGMUX.

By default all flag values <code>{0x00, 0x01, ..., 0xFF}</code> are reserved and cause signature verification to succeed unconditionally on non-empty signatures. This is intended to provide an upgrade path for new digital signature schemes via soft fork. Future BIPs may assign meaning to additional flags.

The below table may be updated as new signature scheme flags are defined.

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Flag !! Signature Scheme !! Specification
|-
| XX || YY || ZZ
|}

===Signature Message===

As in Tapscript leaf version 0xc0, a common signature message and tapscript message extension are computed as specified in BIP 341<ref>https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0341.mediawiki#common-signature-message</ref> and BIP 342<ref>https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0342.mediawiki#user-content-Common_Signature_Message_Extension</ref>.

Within Tapscript CHECKSIGMUX, the ''key_version'' field of the Tapscript message extension is set to the scheme flag byte of the public key being verified against.

Additional domain separation between Tapscript leaf versions 0xc0 and 0xc2 is not required: The leaf version is committed to by the tapleaf hash, both in the output Merkle commitment and in the ''tapleaf_hash'' field of the common signature message extension. Signatures produced for a 0xc2 leaf therefore cannot be replayed against a 0xc0 leaf of the same script tree, or vice versa, and both leaf versions may safely coexist in the same script tree.

===Digital Signature Scheme BIP Requirements===

Future BIPs defining new scheme flags MUST AT MINIMUM specify:

* '''Description''' - Name and description of the signature scheme and links to the reference documents and scheme specification.
* '''Flag byte''' - The constant value assigned to the scheme's flag byte.
* '''Verification procedure''' - Complete algorithm for verification of signatures. Must return success only for valid ''(signature, pubkey, message)'' tuples.
** The verification algorithm must return a positive integer sigops cost to charge against the sigops budget for signature operations (see "Resource Limits"). The sigops cost may be computed based on the actual signature and pubkey being checked. The sigops cost returned for an invalid signature must also be greater than zero. Sigops costs must be <b>a pure function of the signature, pubkey, and message</b>.
* '''Activation Parameters''' - Specifying an activation mechanism and parameters which determine at which block height the flag byte is enforced.

Each signature scheme flag is considered "defined" once deployed by its activation mechanism.

==Resource Limits==

===Stack Element Size===

Tapscript version 0xc0 (BIP 342) limits stack elements to 520 bytes. Tapscript leaf version 0xc2 relaxes the limit on stack element size from 520 bytes to 6000 bytes. This is because some candidate signature schemes produce much larger signatures than BIP 340. Therefore, the new Tapscript version 0xc2 introduced in this BIP raises the 520-byte limit on stack element sizes so as to offer flexibility to support new signature schemes while mitigating denial-of-service risks (see "Design rationale" for discussion).

This limit applies both to elements on the initial stack and to push opcodes within the script.

===Sigops Budget===

In Tapscript leaf version 0xc0, the per-input sigops budget is 50 plus the total serialized size of an input witness in bytes (including the CompactSize prefix). Each signature opcode executed on a non-empty signature decrements this budget by 50; sigops with an empty signature are free. If the cost of a sigop brings the sigops budget below zero, then the script fails immediately.

Tapscript leaf version 0xc2 retains the initial sigops budget of 0xc0, that is 50 plus the serialized size in bytes of the witness. However, version 0xc2 requires signature scheme BIPs to supply a strictly-positive integer cost that is applied to the sigops budget after signature verifications on keys corresponding to that scheme, and defers to these BIPs to justify their chosen cost.

This design allows new signature schemes to be added cleanly as future soft forks. If a new scheme is defined by upgraded nodes, the sigops cost they see is always greater than the sigops cost as seen by a legacy node which is unaware of the new scheme.

Note the precise semantics of sigops debiting differ from Tapscript 0xc0:

* In Tapscript 0xc0:
** The sigops debit amount is a static constant (50).
** The sigops budget is debited for <b>every</b> sigop executed on a non-empty signature.
** The sigops budget is debited and bound-checked <b>before</b> any Schnorr verification is executed.
* In Tapscript CHECKSIGMUX:
** The sigops debit amount is computed deterministically during verification.
** The sigops budget is debited only for non-empty signatures <b>from pubkeys with defined scheme flags</b>.
** The sigops budget is debited and bound-checked <b>after</b> the verification algorithm is executed (so that the cost can be dynamic), but <b>before</b> pushing any results to the stack.

One result of the budget check occurring <i>after</i> verification is that CHECKSIGMUX allows at most <code>50 + len(witness) + S</code> sigops cost per input, where <code>S</code> is the max sigops cost of <b>any</b> defined verification algorithm. Sigops budget failures still take precedence over signature verification failures as in version 0xc0.

==Design Rationale==

===Why Redefine Sigops Rather Than Define New Opcodes?===

An alternative approach would be to redefine separate <code>OP_SUCCESSx</code> opcodes for each new signature scheme. This was rejected for several reasons:

* New signature schemes may be added over time. With scheme flags, adding a new scheme requires only defining a new scheme flag's algorithms and properties. With separate opcodes, each scheme needs its own set of sigop opcodes (<code>OP_CHECKSIG</code>, <code>OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY</code>, <code>OP_CHECKSIGADD</code>), and these do not interoperate naturally in multi-signature constructions.

* Future opcodes involving signature verification, such as <code>OP_CHECKSIGFROMSTACK</code> (BIP 348), can adopt the same flag system, providing consistency across the scripting system. This approach makes inter-BIP compatibility easier to engineer. If this BIP is deployed first, BIP 348 could be updated to support leaf version 0xc2 by following the scheme flag system defined here. Conversely, if BIP 348 is deployed first, this BIP could be updated to also redefine <code>OP_CHECKSIGFROMSTACK</code> in Tapscript leaf version 0xc2.

===Why is the Scheme Flag Part of the Public Key?===

The scheme flag is prepended to the public key rather than to the signature. This ensures the signature scheme is committed to in the tapscript. An attacker who observes a transaction cannot substitute a different scheme and so choose their signature verification function — doing so would require modifying the committed public key in the script.

If the flag were part of the signature, an attacker could control which verification algorithm is used. This would allow theft by specifying a reserved (auto-success) algorithm flag.

The security argument is straightforward: To modify the signature scheme, you must be able to modify the public key that a user commits coins to. If you can modify the public key that a user commits coins to, you can already forge signatures and steal coins.

===Why Raise the Limit on Maximum Stack Element Size?===

Digital signature sizes vary widely. In some schemes the size of a signature is variable, rather than fixed. The signature size cannot always be statically determined from the public key alone. This presents a challenge when deploying new schemes on-chain because the legacy tapscript version 0xc0 restricts stack elements to a maximum size of 520 bytes which some digital signatures may overrun. We could solve this problem in a number of ways.

The simplest approach is to have signatures remain atomic stack elements regardless of their size. However, by doing so, we introduce several new problems and namely, the risk of denial-of-service (DoS) attacks.

Our main DoS concern with raising the stack element size limit would be that someone could construct a script which duplicates and/or hashes a very large stack element many times to consume CPU and/or memory on verifying nodes at relatively low cost. The spender only needs to pay for the script and the initial large stack element. In particular, we identify three DoS attack vectors:

* '''Memory exhaustion.''' Tapscript version 0xc0 permits a stack of up to 1000 elements<ref>https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0342.mediawiki#user-content-Resource_limits</ref>. At the current maximum stack element size of 520 bytes each, a maximum stack size is 520 KB. Allowing larger stack elements (or more of them) scales this resource requirement proportionally. If the maximum stack element size were raised to 6000 bytes the stack could theoretically grow to at most 6 MB of memory with a script like <code><big_data> OP_DUP OP_2DUP OP_3DUP OP_3DUP ... </code>. This memory consumption occurs since a naive implementation of <code>OP_DUP</code> simply duplicates raw bytes in memory.<ref>https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/blob/4f348c2d730ce167d2918727aa2fdb6849182ab1/src/script/interpreter.cpp#L799</ref>

* '''CPU exhaustion.''' Opcodes like <code>OP_RIPEMD160</code> are extremely slow due to a lack of hardware acceleration for the RIPEMD-160 hash function. A script that repeatedly hashes a large element could force significant computation relative to the transaction fees paid, e.g., <code><big_data> OP_DUP OP_2DUP OP_3DUP OP_RIPEMD160 OP_DROP OP_RIPEMD160 OP_DROP ...</code>. Such a script could quickly consume a lot of CPU power on the verifying node, while the spender only has to pay for the cost of the large stack element as an up-front cost. Even at modest fee rates, an attacker could pay a few dollars in transaction fees to force the entire network to run hundreds of thousands of RIPEMD-160 compressions.

* '''Hybrid attacks.''' An attacker could combine duplication and hashing to amplify both memory and CPU costs, e.g., <code><big_data> OP_DUP OP_2DUP OP_3DUP OP_3DUP OP_3DUP ... OP_RIPEMD160 OP_DROP OP_RIPEMD160 OP_DROP ...</code>.

Mitigations exist, and we evaluated three potential solutions to the above attacks:

# '''Opcode Restrictions'''. Forbid <code>DUP</code> opcodes and/or hash opcodes like <code>OP_RIPEMD160</code> from accepting stack elements larger than 520 bytes as input.
# '''Hash caching.''' When verifying a transaction, a node maintains an evicting cache with a fixed max capacity, which maps hash function inputs to outputs. Repeated hashing of the same element hits the cache. This bounds CPU cost to at most one hash per unique element, per scriptable hash function (i.e., SHA1, SHA256, RIPEMD160)<ref>https://en.bitcoin.it/wiki/Script#Crypto</ref>. All further hash invocations on that input would hit the cache.
# '''Pointers / Copy-on-write duplication.''' Node implementations can represent stack elements with pointers or other reference types. Then <code>OP_DUP</code> becomes O(1) rather than O(n), and so duplication of stack elements is cheap. Memory growth is bounded by unique element data, not stack depth. Care must be taken to avoid shared-pointer mutation or use-after-free.

Option (1) addresses both CPU and memory DoS attacks, and doesn't change the status quo from Tapscript version 0xc0. However, it would affect legitimate script authors' ability to work with some types of signatures on the stack the way they can with Schnorr / ECDSA today. It is unclear if use-cases exist for duplicating and/or hashing signatures in script.

Options (2) and (3) are pure implementation changes, have no practical effect on script authors, and are arguably worth implementing independently of this proposal. Together, they address both CPU and memory DoS attacks. To circumvent the hash-cache mitigation, the attacker would either need to pay for the cost of distinct large inputs, or mutate an initial large stack element in script (without making it meaningfully smaller) to trigger a cache-miss, and force the verifier to recompute its hash. The former would be expensive, and the latter is not possible under the current Bitcoin scripting environment.

Note the attacker could implement a hash-chain in script, e.g. <code>OP_RIPEMD160 OP_RIPEMD160 OP_RIPEMD160 ... OP_RIPEMD160 OP_EQUAL</code>; each hash operation would indeed be a cache miss, but only the first hash operation would invoke the full 94 compressions. The rest operate on 20-byte digests.

These mitigations suffice so long as mutation opcodes (<code>OP_CAT</code>, <code>OP_XOR</code>, <code>OP_SUBSTR</code>, etc.) remain disabled. An attacker cannot create many distinct large elements cheaply and each distinct element must be paid for in witness weight.

However, note that if mutation opcodes are enabled in the future, then the caching and pointer mitigations may not suffice. Attackers could cheaply derive new large elements (e.g., appending a byte via <code>OP_CAT</code>), defeating the cache and requiring full re-hashing. Other specific restrictions on duplication and hashing opcodes may be necessary.

Thus, in possession of viable implementation-level mitigations (which are desirable to implement even before this proposal), we opt to relax the stack element size generally, and permit hashing/duplication opcodes to operate on any stack element up to the new limit of 6000 bytes.

===Why Not Allow Chunked Signatures?===

Signatures which exceed the 520-byte stack element limit could be pushed as multiple stack elements of at most 520 bytes each, to be attached to the transaction witness field. These chunks would then be popped and concatenated by sigop opcodes during script evaluation <i>before</i> verifying the signature. This preserves the existing stack element size limit but it introduces complexity. There are several ways to achieve such a chunking mechanism. The chunk count might be fixed per scheme, encoded in the witness, or otherwise derived from transaction or witness structure.

One option for variably sized signature is that sigop opcodes could parse a header from the leading 520 bytes of the signature from the top stack item, use this to determine how long the signature should be, and thus how many stack elements must be popped. This is undesirable, because (1) the script interpreter cannot treat signatures as a black box anymore, and (2) we may open the door to transaction introspection, as the script interpreter must parse and react dynamically to the internal contents of a signature, which is itself bound to the transaction. We would like to maintain a tight scope in our proposal and avoid inadvertently introducing such edge case behavior, and so we discard this option out of caution.

Alternatively, and perhaps more attractively, we could explicitly provide sigop opcodes with the number of signature chunks to pop, by passing an additional witness element as input. This is more stable, but we still face many complex problems, such as:

* '''Static analysis.''' We would be introducing the first Tapscript opcode which pops a variable number of elements from the stack, and that variable number is not typically fixed by the script-pubkey (as it was in legacy <code>OP_CHECKMULTISIG</code>). Instead, the spender can control this, making static script analysis much harder.

* '''Complex off-chain verification.''' Off-chain verifiers (e.g. PSBTs, lightning nodes) can no longer just extract a signature from the witness field of a signed TX and verify it. They must duplicate the consensus logic to parse and concatenate signature chunks before the full reconstructed signature can be verified. Verifiers must also contend with potential adversarial chunking.

* '''Complex edge cases.'''
** We must handle situations where some of the signature chunks are smaller than 520 bytes.
** Some scripts that work fine today would be vulnerable if an attacker could dynamically control how many stack elements to pop (e.g. by popping a pubkey and replacing it with one of their own).
** Other signature schemes with small signature sizes never need signature chunking behavior. How do we handle them?

* '''Precedent.''' From a more philosophical perspective, it is generally bad practice to allow untrusted witness data to tell a verifier how to parse and verify it. A common class of vulnerability originates from this anti-pattern. Deploying chunked signatures sets a questionable precedent for subsequent new signature algorithms and other upgrades to follow the same path.

===Reserved Flags and Forward Compatibility===

Reserved scheme flags cause signature verification to succeed unconditionally. This follows the precedent of unknown public key types in BIP 342, where signature checks against public keys that are not 32 bytes succeed unconditionally so that new public key types can be introduced via soft fork. As with BIP 342, this success applies only to the individual signature check: script execution continues, all other conditions in the script remain enforced, empty signatures still yield the same result as they would for a defined scheme (false for <code>OP_CHECKSIG</code>, script failure for <code>OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY</code>, and no counter increment for <code>OP_CHECKSIGADD</code>). In Tapscript leaf version 0xc2 the sigops budget is <b>not</b> charged for non-empty signatures with reserved flag bytes.

Reserved scheme bytes are strictly for future use and have provisional semantics. Their current behavior, including auto-success and sigops costs, must not be relied upon; a future BIP may redefine it. In particular, there is no cost applied to the sigops budget when verifying against a key with a reserved flag byte, but once defined, those verifications would then be subject to a non-zero cost. Scripts relying upon reserved scheme bytes could become invalid or behave differently after such an upgrade.

Wallets MUST only generate public keys with defined scheme flags, and users MUST verify that their wallets do so. Selecting the correct scheme flag for a keypair is straightforward and easy to validate by inspecting scripts visually.

===Why change ''key_version'' in the signature message?===

The purpose of the ''key_version'' byte in BIP342's message extension is to prevent replay attacks where signatures could be transferred between compatible signature schemes, by ensuring the message for each signature scheme is always distinct. BIP342 did not make direct use of this flag - it is a hardcoded constant in BIP342 - but the BIP anticipated the utility of domain separation if a new signature scheme were to be defined. CHECKSIGMUX makes use of it for the same original purpose.

==Security Considerations==

===Per-Scheme Security===

Each digital signature scheme carries its own security assumptions and potential vulnerabilities. This BIP does not endorse any particular scheme. Security analysis is the responsibility of each new signature scheme proposal.

==Backward Compatibility==

This BIP defines a new Tapscript leaf version that adds new semantics for signature verification and modifies verifier resource constraints. Script semantics for other versions of Tapscript are unaffected. This BIP does not add any output types or modify address encodings. This BIP only affects scripts using leaf version 0xc2 in Tapscript-supporting output types.

Upgraded nodes enforce the rules of this BIP. Non-upgraded nodes treat leaf version 0xc2 as an unknown Tapscript leaf version and, per BIP 341, accept such spends without evaluating the script. The deployment is therefore a soft fork. Non-upgraded nodes will still follow the heaviest, most-worked chain even though they do not validate 0xc2 spending conditions and gain no security from them.

